7.A position is winning if the player whose turn it can force a win with perfect play.
  A position is losing if all the possible moves lead to winning position for the opponent.
  The definition is recursive:start from terminal position,then work backward.If you can move to any losing position,your current position is winning.If every move leads to a winning position fot the opponent then your current position is losing.
8.Terminal positions are game states where the game ends and no further moves are possible.
  Whether a terminal position is a win or a loss depends on the game’s rules.
  Identifying terminal positions is the first step in analyzing a game.
  They act as base cases when working backward through the game tree.
  Examples:
  No opponent pieces left in a capture game.
  Reaching a target node in a graph traversal game.
9.On a DAG, game positions can be solved using backward induction.
  Start from terminal vertices (no outgoing edges).
  Process vertices in reverse topological order.
  For each vertex:
  If any move leads to a losing position → mark it winning.
  If all moves lead to winning positions → mark it losing.
10.Agr ye losing position pe jaa rha hai then mark this current node as winning position and if it is going to a winning position then this is a losing position.
11.DAGs are simple to analyze because they have no cycles.
   You only need:
   A way to mark positions as winning or losing.
   A way to process vertices in reverse topological order.
   Use a boolean array: true = winning, false = losing.
   Process a vertex after all its successors are processed.
   Rule:
   If any outgoing edge leads to a losing position → mark the vertex winning.
   Otherwise → mark it losing.
12.A position is W (winning) if there exists at least one move that puts your opponent in an L (losing) position. Conversely, a position is L if every available move leads to a W position for your opponent. You only need one good move to win.




13.O(n*(n+m))
You are given a directed graph with n nodes and m edges.
Each node represents a game position.
If there is an edge u → v, a player can move from position u to position v.
Two players play alternately.
If a player has no legal move on their turn, they lose (normal play rule).
Both players play optimally.



#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;

    vector<vector<int>> g(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        g[u].push_back(v);
    }

    // 0 = unknown/draw, 1 = win, -1 = lose
    vector<int> state(n, 0);

    // example: terminal nodes are losing
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (g[i].empty()) {
            state[i] = -1;
        }
    }

    bool changed = true;

    while (changed) {
        changed = false;

        for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {
            if (state[u] != 0) continue;

            bool canWin = false;
            bool allWin = true;

            for (int v : g[u]) {
                if (state[v] == -1) canWin = true;
                if (state[v] != 1) allWin = false;
            }

            if (canWin) {
                state[u] = 1;
                changed = true;
            } else if (allWin) {
                state[u] = -1;
                changed = true;
            }
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (state[i] == 1) cout << "WIN\n";
        else if (state[i] == -1) cout << "LOSE\n";
        else cout << "DRAW\n";
    }
}







